# hc-patient-app — what Prettier does not format
#
# Two jobs. The first is the ordinary one: keep generated and vendored files out of scope. The
# second is the deliberate exclusion of `*.html`, which is a decision and not an oversight — read
# the second block before "fixing" it.
#
# This file exists so that a bare `npx prettier --check .` agrees with `npm run prettier:check`.
# Before it, the two disagreed: the npm scripts glob narrowly enough to miss `android/**/build/**`,
# thousands of generated Gradle files that the bare form walks, so they passed by luck rather than
# by design.
#
# An earlier draft of this paragraph also blamed the merged i18n bundles. That was wrong and is
# worth correcting rather than deleting: `src/assets/i18n/*.json` and `src/environments/i18n-hash.ts`
# were always inside the npm glob. They pass because `JSON.stringify(sorted, null, 2)` is already
# Prettier's output shape, not because anything missed them. They are listed below as generated
# output, which is a different and better reason.

# --- deliberate: Angular templates are hand-formatted ------------------------
#
# NO PRETTIER VERSION INDENTS INSIDE ANGULAR CONTROL FLOW. Measured 2026-09-08 against every
# release from 2.8.8 to 4.0.0-alpha.13: all of them flatten the contents of an `@if`/`@for` block
# to the parent's level, so
#
#     @if (item.notes) {                 becomes    @if (item.notes) {
#       <div class="hc-prose">…</div>               <div class="hc-prose">…</div>
#     }                                             }
#
# and a nested block loses its structure entirely. The 12 templates here are indented by hand and
# that is the readable form, so Prettier is pointed away from them rather than allowed to degrade
# them. This is not a stale pin waiting to be bumped: there is no newer version that fixes it, and
# raising the pin changes nothing about this file.
#
# `src/index.html` has no control flow and does conform — it is excluded with the rest so there is
# one rule about HTML here rather than two.
#
# BE CLEAR ABOUT WHAT THIS COSTS: formatting, and only formatting. Template CORRECTNESS is gated,
# as of backlog item 21 (2026-09-08). `.eslintrc.json` has an `*.html` override that parses
# templates with @angular-eslint/template-parser and runs @angular-eslint/eslint-plugin-template
# over them; `npm run lint` is `eslint . --ext .js,.mjs,.ts,.html`; and CI runs `npm run lint`.
# All eleven a11y rules, `banana-in-box`, `no-negated-async` and template `eqeqeq` are at error, and
# `process-inline-templates` extends them to the twelve components in shared/ui that declare their
# markup inline rather than in an .html file. Eleven of the plugin's 29 rules are deliberately off —
# each named in `.eslintrc.json` with its reason and the count still behind it, which is the right
# place for that list rather than here.
#
# TWO LIMITS OF THE PLUGIN, so nobody reads more assurance into the line above than it carries. It is
# @angular-eslint 17 against Angular 20, and it does not visit expressions in `@if`/`@for`/`@switch`
# BLOCK HEADERS, nor the bodies of `@empty` and `@defer`'s `@placeholder`/`@loading`/`@error`. So
# `eqeqeq` is at error and cannot see `@if (a() == 1)`, and a keyboardless `(click)` inside an
# `@empty` block lints clean — measured, not assumed. This repo has 97 `@if` and five `@empty`
# blocks, and `prefer-control-flow` is on, so it actively encourages putting conditions where the
# checks cannot reach. Upgrading the plugin is the fix; it is not this item's.
#
# The paragraph above replaces one that was wrong in both directions on the same subject. Item 16's
# first draft claimed this gate already existed when the plugin was in `devDependencies` and wired
# to nothing; the correction then over-swung and said templates were checked by nothing but the AOT
# compiler, which stopped being true the moment item 21 landed. It is spelled out here rather than
# deleted because this is where somebody comes looking for the reassurance, and the file has now
# misled a reader once in each direction.
*.html

